{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/incompleteness/representability-in-q/composition-representable.tex#proof-2#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/incompleteness/representability-in-q/composition-representable.tex#L57","native_id":"content/incompleteness/representability-in-q/composition-representable.tex#proof-2","kind":"proof","text":"Suppose $h(n) = m$, i.e., $f(g(n)) = m$.  Let $k = g(n)$. Then\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\Th{Q} & \\Proves \\lforall[y][(!A_g(\\num{n}, y) \\lif \\eq[y][\\num{k}])]\n  \\intertext{since $!A_g$ represents~$g$, and}\n  \\Th{Q} & \\Proves \\lforall[z][(!A_f(\\num{k}, z) \\lif \\eq[z][\\num{m}])]\n  \\intertext{since $!A_f$ represents~$f$. Using just a little bit of\n    logic, we can show that also}\n  \\Th{Q} & \\Proves \\lforall[z][(\\lexists[y][(!A_g(\\num{n}, y) \\land\n      !A_f(y, z))] \\lif \\eq[z][\\num{m}])].\n\\end{align*}\ni.e., $\\Th{Q} \\Proves \\lforall[y][(!A_h(\\num n, y) \\lif \\eq[y][\\num m])]$.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"29.8","kind":"proof"}}
{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/lambda-calculus/church-rosser/beta-reduction.tex#lem:str#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/lambda-calculus/church-rosser/beta-reduction.tex#L51","native_id":"content/lambda-calculus/church-rosser/beta-reduction.tex#lem:str","kind":"statement","text":"$\\bred$ is the smallest transitive relation containing $\\bredpar$.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"35.2","kind":"lem"}}
{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/lambda-calculus/syntax/eta.tex#defn-2#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/lambda-calculus/syntax/eta.tex#L50","native_id":"content/lambda-calculus/syntax/eta.tex#defn-2","kind":"definition","name":"Extensionality","text":"We extend the equivalence relation $\\equal$ with the (\\ext) rule:\n  \\begin{center}\n  If $Mx \\equal Nx$ then $M \\equal N$, provided $x \\notin FV(MN)$.\n  \\end{center}\n  and denote the extended relation as $\\equal[\\ext]$.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"38.13","kind":"defn"}}
{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/proof-theory/propositions-as-types/normalization.tex#thm-1#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/proof-theory/propositions-as-types/normalization.tex#L127","native_id":"content/proof-theory/propositions-as-types/normalization.tex#thm-1","kind":"statement","text":"All proof terms reduce to normal form; all !!{derivation}s reduce to\n  normal !!{derivation}s.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"60.4","kind":"thm"}}
